---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { url } from "../lib/paths";
---

<Layout
  title="Contribute to WeCoLab"
  description="Help build a cooperative cloud for people who trust one another with their apps and spare capacity."
>
  <div class="container contribute-page">
    <header class="page-intro contribute-hero">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Come build this with us</p>
        <h1>Good infrastructure starts with people showing up.</h1>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-note">
        <span class="note-label">A note from the workbench</span>
        <p>
          We want the person offering a box, and the person trusting it with an
          app, to understand what happens next. That takes more than code. It
          takes patient docs, awkward failure drills, and people willing to ask
          the obvious question nobody else has asked yet.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/WeCoLabHQ/wecolab">
          Browse the source <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="intro" aria-labelledby="where-title">
      <div class="section-label">01 / Where to begin</div>
      <div>
        <h2 id="where-title">You do not need to start with a controller.</h2>
        <p>
          The project is a Git-backed fabric across sites, with a Go Warden and
          Console, a shell installer, and a Swift app for contributing a Mac.
          There is useful work at every edge where a person meets those pieces.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lanes" aria-label="Ways to contribute">
      <article class="lane">
        <span class="lane-number">01</span>
        <div class="lane-copy">
          <h3>Make the first hour kinder.</h3>
          <p>
            Try the <a href={url("docs/install/")}>install path</a> or the local
            development fabric with fresh eyes. Where does someone have to infer
            what a site, steward or writer means? Improve onboarding language,
            error explanations, diagrams, and the steps that lead from an empty
            box to a first app.
          </p>
        </div>
        <span class="lane-kind">Onboarding / Docs</span>
      </article>
      <article class="lane">
        <span class="lane-number">02</span>
        <div class="lane-copy">
          <h3>Understand the idle Mac.</h3>
          <p>
            The <a href={url("docs/mac/")}>Mac node</a> runs a Debian VM and
            joins its owner's site over Nebula. It reserves a baseline of
            resources, allows more when idle, and makes room again when the
            person returns. Work on its join and reset experience, resource
            reporting, or the awkward transitions between active, idle, sleep
            and disconnect. Idle capacity is not a place for a database.
          </p>
        </div>
        <span class="lane-kind">Swift / Lifecycle</span>
      </article>
      <article class="lane">
        <span class="lane-number">03</span>
        <div class="lane-copy">
          <h3>Rehearse the bad day.</h3>
          <p>
            Walk through a planned move, a lost site, a stale Git copy, or a
            database restore. Record what the operator sees, what must be
            decided by a person, and where the{" "}
            <a href={url("docs/operations/")}>operations guide</a> needs a
            clearer path. A forced move can lose writes not yet archived. Some
            recovery paths described in the docs are still unfinished, so a
            reproducible drill is especially useful.
          </p>
        </div>
        <span class="lane-kind">Recovery / Operations</span>
      </article>
      <article class="lane">
        <span class="lane-number">04</span>
        <div class="lane-copy">
          <h3>Bring an app people actually run.</h3>
          <p>
            Try a small service, then write down its ports, data paths, secrets,
            upgrade steps and limits. App recipes and integrations are better
            when they come from someone who had to live with the app after
            deployment. The <a href={url("docs/console/")}>Console guide</a> is
            a useful starting point.
          </p>
        </div>
        <span class="lane-kind">Apps / Integrations</span>
      </article>
      <article class="lane">
        <span class="lane-number">05</span>
        <div class="lane-copy">
          <h3>Work close to the machinery.</h3>
          <p>
            The Go code reconciles site and app state from Git, handles roles
            and moves, and refuses destructive steps without evidence. The Swift
            code owns the Mac VM and its host-guest boundary. If either is your
            kind of work, start with a behavior you can explain and reproduce
            rather than a sweeping rewrite. The{" "}
            <a href={url("docs/security/")}>security model</a> is worth reading
            before changing a trust boundary.
          </p>
        </div>
        <span class="lane-kind">Go / Swift</span>
      </article>
    </section>

    <section class="start" aria-labelledby="start-title">
      <div class="section-label">02 / Run it on your machine</div>
      <div class="start-main">
        <h2 id="start-title">A whole fabric, on one laptop.</h2>
        <p>
          The <a href={url("docs/development/")}>development guide</a> describes
          the code, tests and a Docker-based fabric: a public site, a home site
          behind a simulated NAT, a laptop node and an object-storage vault. It
          needs about 15 GB free in Docker's disk. The same installer runs
          inside those containers as on real boxes, with documented
          substitutions for things a laptop cannot provide.
        </p>
        <div
          class="command-block"
          aria-label="Commands from the development guide"
        >
          <div class="command-head">
            <span>From the repository root</span>
            <span>docs/development.md</span>
          </div>
          <pre><code>make dev-up
make dev-test
make dev-down</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p class="command-caption">
          These are the guide's setup, end-to-end check and teardown commands.
          Read it first for the environment, what the checks cover, and how to
          reload code at each site while developing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="closing" aria-labelledby="next-title">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">A good next step</p>
        <h2 id="next-title">Bring a question. Bring a rough edge.</h2>
        <p>
          Read the source, look through the issues, and describe what you tried
          and what happened. A small, careful contribution that makes the next
          person's day easier belongs here.
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button" href="https://github.com/WeCoLabHQ/wecolab/issues">
            Explore issues <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
          </a>
          <a
            class="button button-secondary"
            href="https://github.com/WeCoLabHQ/wecolab"
          >
            View repository <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <aside class="license-note">
        <span class="note-label">Built in the open</span>
        <p>
          WeCoLab is released under the{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/WeCoLabHQ/wecolab/blob/main/LICENSE">
            GNU Affero General Public License v3.0
          </a>
          . You can study and change the code. If you run a modified version for
          other people, the license requires you to offer them its source.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .contribute-page {
    padding-bottom: 6rem;
  }
  .contribute-hero {
    padding: clamp(4.5rem, 9vw, 9rem) 0 5rem;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.5fr) minmax(270px, 0.7fr);
    gap: clamp(2rem, 7vw, 8rem);
    align-items: end;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ink);
  }
  .contribute-hero h1 {
    max-width: 900px;
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    font-size: clamp(3.4rem, 7.3vw, 7.2rem);
    line-height: 0.99;
    letter-spacing: -0.065em;
    font-weight: 500;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .hero-note {
    border-top: 3px solid var(--accent);
    padding-top: 1.25rem;
  }
  .note-label,
  .section-label,
  .lane-number,
  .lane-kind,
  .command-head {
    font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace;
    font-size: 0.76rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .note-label {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .hero-note p {
    font-size: 1.12rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin: 1.2rem 0;
  }
  .intro,
  .start {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(165px, 25%) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 2rem;
    padding: 4.5rem 0;
  }
  .section-label {
    color: var(--accent);
    padding-top: 0.65rem;
  }
  .intro h2,
  .start h2,
  .closing h2 {
    font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 4vw, 4.2rem);
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.08;
    letter-spacing: -0.05em;
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .intro p,
  .start-main > p {
    max-width: 760px;
    font-size: 1.08rem;
    line-height: 1.74;
  }
  .contribute-page a:not(.button):not(.button-secondary) {
    color: var(--accent);
    text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
  }
  .lanes {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--ink);
  }
  .lane {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(58px, 12%) minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(125px, 19%);
    gap: 1.2rem;
    padding: 2.2rem 0 2.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .lane-number {
    color: var(--accent);
    padding-top: 0.5rem;
  }
  .lane h3 {
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 2.8vw, 2.5rem);
    line-height: 1.18;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
  }
  .lane p {
    max-width: 690px;
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1.72;
  }
  .lane-kind {
    justify-self: end;
    color: var(--muted);
    padding-top: 0.5rem;
    text-align: right;
  }
  .start {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ink);
  }
  .command-block {
    max-width: 760px;
    background: var(--ink);
    color: #e7ede5;
    margin: 2.2rem 0 1rem;
  }
  .command-head {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding: 1rem 1.4rem;
    color: #a8c9be;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #48605b;
  }
  pre {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.4rem 1.55rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", monospace;
    font-size: clamp(0.93rem, 1.3vw, 1.05rem);
    line-height: 1.95;
  }
  pre code {
    font: inherit;
  }
  .start-main .command-caption {
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .closing {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(230px, 300px);
    gap: clamp(2rem, 7vw, 7rem);
    padding-top: 4.5rem;
    align-items: end;
  }
  .closing h2 {
    max-width: 720px;
  }
  .closing p:not(.eyebrow) {
    max-width: 650px;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }
  .actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.85rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }
  .license-note {
    padding: 1.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .license-note p {
    margin-bottom: 0;
    font-size: 0.94rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .contribute-hero {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      padding-top: 4rem;
    }
    .hero-note {
      max-width: 610px;
    }
    .intro,
    .start {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 1rem;
      padding: 3.5rem 0;
    }
    .section-label {
      padding-top: 0;
    }
    .lane {
      grid-template-columns: 45px 1fr;
    }
    .lane-kind {
      grid-column: 2;
      justify-self: start;
      text-align: left;
      padding: 0.2rem 0 0;
    }
    .closing {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
    .license-note {
      max-width: 500px;
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 500px) {
    .contribute-hero h1 {
      font-size: clamp(3.1rem, 12.5vw, 4.5rem);
    }
    .lane {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 0.4rem;
    }
    .lane-number,
    .lane-kind {
      grid-column: 1;
    }
    .lane-kind {
      padding-top: 0.4rem;
    }
    .command-head {
      flex-wrap: wrap;
    }
  }
</style>
